--TEST--
`rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop`'s connection loop compiles as written, and `Core\Socket::current()` is the one place a program that is not a connection isolate is refused — `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-root-isolate` and `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1 gives a connection its own root isolate and moves the socket into it, so
// the peer belongs to the isolate and not to any value a program holds. § 3's
// script reads it back through `current()`, which is therefore the one member
// that can tell a connection from everything else: an ordinary request, a
// `spawn script` child and this command-line run all reach the same refusal,
// because none of them was handed a socket.
//
// The loop below is § 3's, written out so that its *shape* is pinned by a case
// that can run anywhere -- `receive()` answers one message from either source,
// `topic()` is what tells a bus delivery from a peer frame, and `text()`,
// `bytes()` and `value()` are what each of the two kinds carries.
class Chat {
    public static function describe(Core\Socket\Message $msg): string {
        if ($msg->topic() != null) {
            return "a delivery";
        }
        if ($msg->value() != null) {
            return "carrying a value";
        }
        if ($msg->text() != null) {
            return "a text frame";
        }
        if ($msg->bytes() != null) {
            return "a binary frame";
        }
        return "an empty frame";
    }
}

try {
    var $conn = Core\Socket::current();
    var $msg = $conn->receive();
    while ($msg != null) {
        echo Chat::describe($msg), "\n";
        $conn->send("ack");
        $conn->sendBytes("ack" as bytes);
        $msg = $conn->receive();
    }
    echo "the peer closed\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
`Core\Socket::current` needs a connection and this program is not one: only a script `Core\Socket::upgrade` opened has one
